begin;

select public.seed_cipmn_mock_module('CIPMN-MOD-003','[["Selecting a project life-cycle model","The team has not decided whether delivery should be predictive, iterative, Agile or hybrid, even though requirement uncertainty and release needs are already known.","Evaluate uncertainty, stakeholder feedback needs, dependency structure and governance constraints, then select and tailor the most suitable life-cycle model.","Choose the life cycle alphabetically so the decision appears neutral.","Use Waterfall for every project because it is easiest to document.","Start execution without a life-cycle model and decide after the first major delay.","Life-cycle selection should reflect the nature of the work, uncertainty, value-delivery cadence, dependencies and governance requirements."],["Stage-Gate control","A project moves from concept to design and procurement without confirming feasibility, business justification or readiness at defined decision points.","Establish stage gates with evidence-based entry and exit criteria and authorised go, hold, revise or stop decisions.","Allow automatic progression whenever the calendar reaches the next phase.","Use gates only to review staff attendance.","Wait until closure to determine whether earlier stages were complete.","Stage-Gate control supports disciplined investment decisions by verifying readiness and continued justification before progression."],["Work Breakdown Structure as a planning tool","The schedule is built from a list of activities that cannot be traced to approved deliverables, and several scope elements are missing.","Create a deliverable-oriented WBS that applies the 100 percent rule, then derive work packages and activities from it.","Add more dates to the current list without linking work to scope.","Use the organisational chart as the WBS because departments perform the work.","Replace the WBS with a stakeholder register.","A WBS decomposes the total approved scope into manageable deliverables and work packages, providing a foundation for schedule, cost and responsibility planning."],["Milestone planning","Executives need a concise view of key decision, approval and delivery points, but the detailed schedule contains thousands of activities.","Develop a milestone plan linked to the detailed schedule and define objective completion criteria for each milestone.","Delete the detailed schedule and manage only with milestone names.","Treat every routine task as a milestone.","Set milestone dates without linking them to underlying work.","Milestones provide high-level control points but must remain connected to detailed activity logic and clear acceptance conditions."],["Gantt chart and network diagram use","The team can see activity dates on a Gantt chart but cannot explain the dependency logic or why a delay affects later work.","Use a network diagram to validate dependencies and critical logic, while retaining the Gantt chart for time-phased communication.","Remove dependencies and retain only coloured bars.","Use the risk matrix to calculate activity sequence.","Assume activities are independent because they appear on separate rows.","Gantt charts communicate timing, while network diagrams expose logical relationships, paths and dependency effects."],["Critical Path Method","A noncritical activity is delayed by three days but has five days of total float, while management assumes the project completion date must move.","Confirm the activity''s float and current network effects before changing the completion forecast.","Move the project completion date by three days automatically.","Declare every delayed activity critical.","Ignore the delay without updating the schedule.","A delay within available float may not affect project completion; CPM analysis should determine the actual impact."],["PERT and three-point estimation","Activity duration is highly uncertain, and the team has credible optimistic, most likely and pessimistic estimates.","Use a three-point or PERT estimate to represent uncertainty rather than relying on a single unsupported duration.","Use only the optimistic estimate to motivate the team.","Average the highest cost and lowest cost instead of duration estimates.","Set the duration equal to the sponsor''s preferred date.","PERT-style three-point estimating incorporates a range of plausible durations and supports more realistic schedule analysis."],["Earned Value Management","At the status date, the project has spent more than planned but the team cannot tell how much value of work has actually been completed.","Compare planned value, earned value and actual cost, then calculate schedule and cost performance indicators.","Compare actual cost only with the total approved budget.","Assume high spending means high progress.","Use stakeholder satisfaction as the only schedule metric.","EVM integrates scope, schedule and cost by comparing planned value, earned value and actual cost."],["Resource allocation and levelling","The same crane is assigned to two simultaneous activities, creating an impossible schedule even though the logic otherwise appears valid.","Level or reallocate the constrained resource and recalculate the schedule and critical path.","Keep both assignments because software permits overlapping entries.","Remove the crane from the resource plan without changing the work method.","Increase the budget baseline while leaving the conflict unresolved.","Resource feasibility must be integrated into the schedule; levelling may alter dates and criticality."],["Time-cost trade-off and crashing","A contractual deadline must be recovered, and management proposes adding expensive resources to every remaining activity.","Analyse critical activities, compare crash cost per unit of time and accelerate only where feasible and cost-effective.","Crash activities with large float first because they are easier to change.","Add resources without checking productivity, safety or diminishing returns.","Reduce quality requirements without approval to save time.","Crashing is a deliberate time-cost trade-off focused on critical-path activities where added cost can genuinely shorten duration."],["Project dashboards and variance reporting","A dashboard shows only green status indicators even though underlying schedule and cost variances are worsening.","Use objective thresholds, source data, trends, forecasts and explanatory commentary so status reflects actual performance.","Keep indicators green to protect stakeholder confidence.","Remove variance figures because executives may misinterpret them.","Report only activities completed, regardless of baseline or forecast.","A useful dashboard is evidence-based, transparent and decision-oriented, not a cosmetic presentation."],["Risk tools and risk register","Risk information is scattered across emails, with no owner, probability, impact, response or review date.","Consolidate risks in a structured register, analyse and prioritise them, assign owners and track responses and triggers.","Archive the emails and assume the risks are controlled.","Record only the risk titles without response information.","Assign every risk to the project manager regardless of control authority.","A risk register supports ownership, analysis, response planning, monitoring and communication."],["Quantitative risk analysis","Decision-makers need to understand the probability of meeting a cost target under multiple interacting uncertainties.","Use an appropriate quantitative technique such as Monte Carlo simulation with defensible input distributions and assumptions.","Use a simple colour code as the only evidence of cost confidence.","Choose the lowest possible estimate and call it the expected value.","Average unrelated stakeholder opinions without modelling uncertainty.","Quantitative analysis models the combined effect of uncertainty and can estimate confidence levels for cost or schedule outcomes."],["Stakeholder identification and analysis","A regulator with high influence and urgent information needs is treated the same as a low-interest observer.","Analyse stakeholders by influence, interest, impact and needs, then tailor engagement and communication accordingly.","Send the same generic message to every stakeholder at the same frequency.","Engage only stakeholders who support the project.","Rank stakeholders solely by job title.","Stakeholder analysis enables differentiated engagement based on power, interest, impact, legitimacy and information needs."],["RACI, communication matrix and escalation paths","Two managers believe they are accountable for the same approval, team members receive conflicting instructions and unresolved decisions have no escalation route.","Clarify responsibility and single-point accountability with a RACI, define communication arrangements and establish time-bound escalation paths.","Allow both managers to remain accountable so responsibility is shared.","Ask team members to choose which instruction to follow.","Escalate every routine matter directly to the chief executive.","RACI clarifies roles, communication planning defines information flow and escalation paths ensure unresolved matters reach the right authority."]]'::jsonb);

commit;
